      "message": "Resolving the other comments here, spent a little time thinking about the best way to implemen t this in QUADS 3.0 given the newer design and wanting to move away from legacy scripts.\n\nI think it\u0027s best this is implemented directly as part of --modify-switch-conf.\n\nI\u0027ve got a full implementation checklist/guide for you here:  https://gist.github.com/sadsfae/9d29938118eb3aa1897374c9c80d34f5\n\nI\u0027ll provide a basic summary below, and I think we can aim to have this land in the 3.0.x train after we get 3.0 released.\n\n\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d tiny bit of targeted summary slop here \u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\u003d\n\n1. The feature becomes an operation of --mod-switch-conf, the existing\n   ad-hoc switch configuration command. No new standalone tool is shipped.\n\n2. The new CLI surface is:\n\n     quads --mod-switch-conf --roce --roce-action install --sw \u003cip\u003e\n     quads --mod-switch-conf --roce --roce-action install --sw-list \u003cfile\u003e\n     quads --mod-switch-conf --roce --roce-action uninstall --sw \u003cip\u003e\n     quads --mod-switch-conf --roce --roce-action uninstall --sw-list \u003cfile\u003e\n     quads --mod-switch-conf --roce --roce-action configure --host \u003ch\u003e --interfaces em1,em3 [--dry-run]\n     quads --mod-switch-conf --roce --roce-action remove    --host \u003ch\u003e --interfaces em1,em3 [--dry-run]\n\n   --roce is the primary selector. --roce-action takes one of the four\n   operation names as its value. --host and --dry-run are reused unchanged.\n\n3. Placement mirrors the dayzero repository structure but not the dayzero\n   runtime:\n\n   - Vendor library moves verbatim to plugins/builtin/roce/juniper.py. It is\n     a library package, not a plugin category; no discovery change is needed.\n   - SwitchPlugin gains one non-abstract roce() method (default returns False\n     so external site plugins keep loading; an abstract method would break\n     them silently).\n   - SwitchDispatcher gains a roce() passthrough.\n   - JuniperSwitchPlugin implements roce() using the moved library; a second\n     switch vendor is a second plugin, not a second dispatch site.\n\n4. Why not dayzero as runtime: dayzero runs once per released cloud from\n   tools/notify.py, is cloud-level only, is non-fatal by design, and cannot be\n   re-run or see host/interface context. RoCE install is switch-level and\n   configure/remove is per-host at allocation time. The validator category\n   (operator-invoked via --validate-env) is the real precedent for\n   deterministic operator-run actions.\n\nKEY POINTS TO SIGNAL IN REVIEW\n\n5. The unrelated badfish delta that was in earlier revisions is gone:\n   commit 7b26bcb (iDRAC \"already committed\" fix) is now merged into\n   development, and the current revision sits directly on it. Effective patch\n   surface is only the four RoCE files. Nothing further to split.\n\n6. One naming trap: Interface.vendor is the NIC vendor (from lshw), not the\n   switch vendor. Do not dispatch the switch CLI dialect on it. The active\n   switch plugin is the honest switch-vendor signal.\n\n7. The commit message claims a \"verify\" action that does not exist. The\n   implementation has exactly install/uninstall/configure/remove. Correct the\n   message.\n\n8. The design keeps everything additive: no schema migration (the roce_capable\n   field is deferred; a NIC-vendor gate or an explicit operator flag covers the\n   need), no new plugin category, no dayzero wiring, no dispatcher model\n   change. That is what keeps 3.0 testing unperturbed.\n\nWHAT THE IMPLEMENTATION GUIDE (section 7) ASKS THE AUTHOR TO DO\n\n9. Verify the patch is rebased on current development first.\n\n10. git-mv the vendor library to plugins/builtin/roce/ keeping the Junos\n    command tables byte-identical.\n\n11. Add the non-abstract SwitchPlugin.roce() default and the dispatcher\n    passthrough.\n\n12. Add the parser arguments, plus a one-line gate in cli/main.py so that\n    --roce without --mod-switch-conf errors instead of being silently ignored\n    by the default command view.\n\n13. Branch action_mod_switch_conf on --roce and implement the validation\n    matrix: RoCE and --change/--nicN are mutually exclusive; install/uninstall\n    need exactly one of --sw or --sw-list; configure/remove need --host and\n    --interfaces. Validation errors exit 2 like all other missing-option\n    cases.\n\n14. Return integer exit codes (0 success, 1 failure, raise on validation);\n    do not return a bool. The current legacy branch already discards the\n    dispatcher return value, and bool is an int subclass, so a naive return\n    would make failures exit 0. This is called out as a real bug to avoid\n    copying.\n\n15. Port the tests to tests/plugins/test_switch_roce.py. Keep the valuable\n    cases: idempotent skip, base-config required, per-switch session reuse,\n    interface-name validation, dry-run with no SSH, continue on per-interface\n    error, and multi-switch partial failure (one switch fails, the other still\n    applied). Replace the failure tests that count expect() calls with\n    command-identity injection (raise on the \"commit\" sendline).\n\n16. Rewrite docs/quads-roce-setup.md for the quads CLI, add a deprecation note\n    mapping each old standalone command to its replacement, update README, and\n    fix the commit message.\n\nGUARD RAILS (section 7, \"allowed\" list)\n\n17. The final diff is limited to: cli/parser.py, cli/main.py, cli/cli.py,\n    plugins/interfaces/switch.py, plugins/dispatchers/switch.py,\n    plugins/builtin/switches/juniper.py, the new plugins/builtin/roce/\n    package, tests/plugins/test_switch_roce.py plus tests/cli additions,\n    docs/quads-roce-setup.md and README.md, and the three deletions\n    (tools/roce.py, tools/external/juniper_roce.py, tests/tools/test_roce.py).\n\n18. Forbidden in this change: models/migrations, discovery/app wiring,\n    dayzero/notify, structural conf/plugins.yml changes, keeping the\n    standalone tool as a shim, touching the legacy --change/--nicN behavior.\n\nOPEN QUESTIONS FOR THE AUTHOR/REVIEWERS\n\n19. Flag surface: locked as --roce (primary, store_true) plus --roce-action\n    with choices install/uninstall/configure/remove. One choices\u003d argument was\n    chosen over four secondary --roce-* flags because it matches the existing\n    sub-mode idiom (--export, --qinq, --boot-mode), keeps help/docs/tests at a\n    single argument, extends to future operations by adding one choice string,\n    and gets invalid-value rejection from argparse for free. The action branch\n    reads the same roce_action value regardless, so this is a parser-only\n    decision. The four-flag form remains an acceptable alternative if a\n    reviewer prefers it, the implementation is unaffected.\n\n20. Exit-code behavior for the existing --change/--nicN path: it currently\n    exits 0 even when the switch plugin fails. The document fixes this for the\n    RoCE branch only and leaves the legacy behavior unchanged. If reviewers\n    want the legacy path fixed too, that is a one-line follow-up, not part of\n    this change.\n\n21. The docs\u0027 earlier \"update roce.py to select the implementation based on\n    switch vendor\" instruction is obsolete: vendor selection is the plugin\n    itself. If a second vendor joins later, the plan adds an explicit\n    --switch-plugin selection knob (post-3.0) but never Interface.vendor\n    routing.\n\nSUMMARY FOR A REVIEWER\n\n22. Functionality is sound and worth keeping; the change wants to relocate it,\n    not rewrite it. The three architectural objections to the current shape:\n    standalone tool in the legacy tools/ area, god-class orchestration, and a\n    docs-specified third dispatch site. The proposed shape resolves all three\n    with additive changes only. Approve the direction; review the\n    implementation section as the checklist.",
